{"name":"Carnation Bouquet","hex":"#f5c0d0","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"carnation","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A type of Eurasian plant widely cultivated for its flowers.\noriginally, Dianthus caryophyllus\nother members of genus Dianthus and hybrids","originally, Dianthus caryophyllus"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a rosy pink or red colour.","Of a human flesh color."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/carnation"},{"term":"bouquet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An arrangement or bunch of cut flowers; a nosegay.","A decoratively arranged bunch of something."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bouquet"}],"entries":[{"title":"Dianthus caryophyllus","blocks":[{"text":"Dianthus caryophyllus, commonly known as carnation or clove pink, is a species of Dianthus native to the Mediterranean region. While its exact natural range is uncertain due to extensive cultivation over the last 2,000 years, wild carnations are most common in the Mediterranean region. Carnations are prized for their wide variety of colors, delicate fringed petals, and fragrance, often described as spicy, clove-like, or reminiscent of a combination of cinnamon and nutmeg, hence the common name \"clove pink\". This aroma has made carnations a popular choice for use in perfumes, potpourri, and scented products. Culturally, carnations are associated with affection, distinction, and maternal love, with variations depending on color and area within its wide geographical range."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/W_carnation4051.jpg/330px-W_carnation4051.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dianthus_caryophyllus","wikidata":"Q158984"},{"title":"Shades of pink","blocks":[{"text":"Pink colors are usually light or desaturated shades of reds, roses, and magentas which are created on computer and television screens using the RGB color model and in printing with the CMYK color model. As such, it is an arbitrary classification of color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Color_icon_pink_v2.svg/330px-Color_icon_pink_v2.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_pink","wikidata":"Q19847189"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Stilleben._Frukt._Binoit_-_Skoklosters_slott_-_22175.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Stilleben._Frukt._Binoit_-_Skoklosters_slott_-_22175.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Stilleben._Frukt._Binoit_-_Skoklosters_slott_-_22175.tif/lossy-page1-1280px-Stilleben._Frukt._Binoit_-_Skoklosters_slott_-_22175.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Stilleben._Frukt._Binoit_-_Skoklosters_slott_-_22175.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Stilleben._Frukt._Binoit_-_Skoklosters_slott_-_22175.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Peter Binoit, Stilleben, frukt – Still life with carnations, 1618","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStilleben._Frukt._Binoit_-_Skoklosters_slott_-_22175.tif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Photo_Women_collecting_carnations_1962_-_Touring_Club_Italiano_1.2410.jpg/500px-Photo_Women_collecting_carnations_1962_-_Touring_Club_Italiano_1.2410.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Photo_Women_collecting_carnations_1962_-_Touring_Club_Italiano_1.2410.jpg/1280px-Photo_Women_collecting_carnations_1962_-_Touring_Club_Italiano_1.2410.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https: